# BUGS

Newly discovered bugs go at the end of this file (see CLAUDE.md, "Track Newly Discovered Bugs").
Do not fix unrelated bugs inline without asking first.

Entry template:

## [YYYY-MM-DD] Short title
- Where: file/module/component
- What: what's wrong, observed vs expected behavior
- How found: what you were doing when you noticed it
- Severity: low / medium / high
- Status: open

## [2026-08-07] RenderProject crashed reading `svgElement.querySelector('#bg').children` — FIXED
- Where: main.js:1348-1351 (`RenderProject`): `if(svgElement) { let viewBox = ...; let bgRects = [...svgElement.querySelector('#bg').children]; ... }`
- What: `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'children')` on every document load. `svgElement.querySelector('#bg')` returned `null` because the live renderer (`eagle-renderer.js`'s `EagleSVGRenderer.renderSVG()`) emits `Background`'s `<rect>` and `Grid`'s `<rect>` as direct children of the `<svg>` root, not wrapped in a `<g id="bg">` — that wrapper only exists in the dead legacy renderer (`lib/eagle/components/drawing.js:47`, `h('g', { id: 'bg', ... }, ...)`), which nothing live uses anymore. Same class of stale-API-contract mismatch already tracked in TODO.md item 5.
- How found: fixing the `EagleMaps`/`project.renderer.maps` crash and the `SaveSVG`/`ElementToXML` crash (both below, already fixed) let `RenderProject` run far enough to reach this line for the first time — it was previously masked because `EagleMaps` aborted `RenderProject` before execution ever got here.
- Fix: confirmed live (via `svg.children`) that the new renderer emits exactly two direct-child `<rect>` elements under the `<svg>` root (Background's and Grid's) ahead of the `<g class="drawing">`. Replaced the `#bg`-lookup with `svgElement.querySelectorAll(':scope > rect')`, which selects the same two rects without depending on the dead wrapper `<g id="bg">`. Verified live: `window.viewBox` is now a working live object and `RenderProject` proceeds past this line.
- Status: closed

## [2026-08-07] RenderProject crashed on `doc.getBounds is not a function` — FIXED
- Where: main.js:1379-1397 (`RenderProject`): `let { name, data, doc, svg, bbox } = project; let bounds = doc.getBounds(); let rect = bounds.toRect(Rect.prototype); ...; let measures = (doc.measures || doc.getBounds()).rect;`
- What: `TypeError: doc.getBounds is not a function` on every document load. `EagleDocument` (`lib/eagle-dom.js`) has no `getBounds()` method (nor `.measures`) — another stale-API mismatch against the new DOM model (same class as the two entries above). Uncovered only after the `#bg`/`bgRects` crash right above it was fixed and `RenderProject` reached this block for the first time.
- How found: live repro via `LoadDocument(...)` after fixing the `#bg` crash.
- Fix: this block only ever used `doc.getBounds()`'s result to derive `rect.width`/`rect.height` (a zero-bounds gate) — the renderer (`eagle-renderer.js`) already computes the document's real bounding box internally and writes it to the rendered `<svg>`'s `viewBox` attribute, so that's a strictly better source of truth than re-deriving bounds from the doc. Replaced `doc.getBounds().toRect(...)` with `new Rect(svgElement.getAttribute('viewBox').split(/\s+/g))` (same technique already used a few lines above for `globalThis.viewBox`), and `(doc.measures || doc.getBounds()).rect` with `doc.measures || rect` (that `measures` var was already dead — never read after assignment — so this only needed to stop throwing). Verified live for both `.sch` and `.brd` documents: `RenderProject` now completes with zero console errors, `data-filename`/`data-aspect` attributes are set correctly on the `<svg>`.
- Status: closed

## [2026-08-07] EagleMaps threw on `project.renderer.maps.map` (undefined) — FIXED
- Where: main.js:976-982, `EagleMaps()`
- What: loading any board/schematic threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` because `project.renderer.maps` doesn't exist on the new `eagle-renderer.js` `EagleSVGRenderer`/`SchematicRenderer`/etc. (only the dead legacy `lib/eagle/svgRenderer.js` had a `.maps` getter). `project.doc.maps` was likewise never defined on `EagleDocument` (`lib/eagle-dom.js`). Confirmed via live browser repro (`LoadDocument(...)`) that nothing downstream in `main.js`/`commands.js`/`lib/eagle/components*` ever actually reads `project.maps.*` — the whole `EagleMaps()` output is presently write-only/unconsumed. This crash also aborted `RenderProject` before it reached the lines that set `project.svgElement`, which was the direct cause of the SaveSVG/ElementToXML crash below.
- Fix: guarded the two destructures with fallbacks (`project.renderer.maps ? ... : [noopMap, noopMap]`, and default values on `path2eagle`/`eagle2path` from `project.doc.maps ?? {}`) so `EagleMaps()` no longer throws when the new renderer/doc don't populate `.maps`. This is a stopgap — implementing real `.maps` tracking on the new renderer (mirroring the old `EagleSVGRenderer`'s `data-path`-keyed `path2component`/`component2path`) is future work, already scoped under TODO.md item 5's renderer-API migration.
- Status: closed

## [2026-08-07] SaveSVG/ElementToXML called XMLSerializer.serializeToString with a non-Node argument — FIXED
- Where: main.js:362-363 (`ElementToXML`), called from `SaveSVG` (main.js:470-471), called from `main.js:1256-1257`
- What: `TypeError: Failed to execute 'serializeToString' on 'XMLSerializer': parameter 1 is not of type 'Node'.` on every document load. Root cause: not an independent bug — `RenderProject` schedules `setTimeout(() => SaveSVG(), 500)` and *then* crashes in `EagleMaps()` (previous entry) before reaching the later line that assigns `project.svgElement`. 500ms later the scheduled `SaveSVG()` reads `project.svgElement`, finds it `undefined`, and passes that into `ElementToXML` → `XMLSerializer.serializeToString(undefined)`.
- Fix: none needed directly — fixing the `EagleMaps` crash above let `RenderProject` reach the `project.svgElement = Element.find('svg', element)` assignment before the `SaveSVG` timeout fires. Verified live: `project.svgElement` is now a real `<svg>` node and `SaveSVG`/`ElementToXML` no longer throw.
- Status: closed

## [2026-07-23] RETRACTED — not a bug: WS clients must send Sec-WebSocket-Protocol for mount dispatch to work
- Where: previously filed against qjs-lws (lws.so) mount->protocol dispatch
- What it looked like: a `LWSContext` with several `mounts` pointing at different `protocol` names always seemed to dispatch WS upgrades to `protocols[0]` once a second `createServer()`/`LWSContext` existed anywhere in the process, regardless of which mountpoint the client connected to.
- Actual root cause (found by reading libwebsockets' own `lws_process_ws_upgrade()` in `qjs-lws/libwebsockets/lib/roles/ws/server-ws.c`): when a WS client sends no `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header at all, libwebsockets binds the connection to `vhost->default_protocol_index` (`protocols[0]`) unconditionally and never consults mount data — this is correct, RFC 6455 §4.2.2-compliant behavior, nothing to do with context count. My test clients happened to omit that header in every case that "failed"; my JSON/raw ports "worked" only because they're each the sole (and thus already-index-0) protocol on their context. Confirmed by re-running the exact same multi-context, multi-mount setup with a client that sends `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: browser` (matching qjs-lws's own `examples/debugger/demo.js`, which already does this unmodified) — dispatch was correct every time, context count and creation order both irrelevant.
- Fix: none needed in qjs-lws. `quickjs/qjs-debugger/qjs-debugger.js` (`StartServer`) and `connector-lws.js` were reverted from the single-mount workaround back to the normal multi-mount design (matching `server.js`), and `LWSConnector`'s 'browser'-mode protocol is explicitly named `'browser'` to match what `demo.js` requests.
- Status: closed (retracted)

## [2026-08-26] ../pictest/src/miditest.md §8.0 pin table: JP1-4 paired with wrong DIP/port pin
- Where: `../pictest/src/miditest.md` §8.0 "Finalized pin table", the `LCD_RESET` row: `| JP1-4 / RA3 (DIP 5) | LCD LCD_RESET | ...`
- What: `../pictest/picstick.md` §1's own JP1 table (cross-checked against the real `picstick_25k50_v1.sch` netlist) says header pin JP1-4 = RA2/DIP4 (AN2/VREF-), and RA3/DIP5 is actually header pin JP1-5, not JP1-4. `miditest.md`'s row conflates the two — pairing header pin "JP1-4" with port/DIP pin "RA3 (DIP 5)", which belong to different header pins. Found while building a combined EAGLE schematic (`eagle-circuit` skill) that wires directly to real `PICSTICK` header pins per this table — the mismatch would have sent `LCD_RESET` to the wrong physical pin (RA2 or RA3, depending which field is trusted) had it not been caught before generating the `.scr`.
- Fix: not applied to `miditest.md` (outside this repo, and the user resolved the underlying design question differently — see below) — this entry just records the doc bug for whoever edits `miditest.md` next.
- Status: open (worked around, not fixed at the source) — the actual harness design moved `LCD_RESET` off this pin entirely, onto JP2-2/RB7 (see the same doc's own "still free" list), sidestepping the ambiguity rather than resolving which of JP1-4/JP1-5 was intended.

## [2026-08-26] eagle-verify.sh intermittently reports FAIL/timeout on content that runs cleanly
- Where: `~/.claude/skills/eagle-circuit/eagle-verify.sh` (headless EAGLE run wrapper used by the eagle-circuit skill)
- What: running the exact same unmodified `.scr` through this script repeatedly gives inconsistent results — sometimes "FAIL: ... timed out or crashed" with an empty `eagle.log` and no dialog windows ever seen, sometimes "PASS". Manually reproducing the same invocation the script performs (`Xvfb` + `eagle -N- -S<script> <out.sch>`, polled directly rather than through this wrapper) consistently succeeds in well under 20s for the same content, every time. This was mistaken for a real "reorder this net's member list and EAGLE hangs" bug in an earlier session, and re-chased as such this session too, before manual invocation proved several "hanging" variants actually complete fine — the wrapper itself, not EAGLE or the schematic content, is what's unreliable.
- How found: debugging a reported wire-vs-component-body crossing in `.tmp/picstick-combined.circuit.json` — needed to alternate between `eagle-verify.sh` and manual EAGLE invocations across several edits, and the same content flipped between FAIL and PASS across consecutive runs with no change to the `.scr` and no leftover `Xvfb`/`eagle` processes or disk pressure at the time of either result.
- Not yet root-caused: candidates not yet checked include a race between the `Xvfb "$DISPLAY_NUM"` background start and the `sleep 2` before launching `eagle` (2s may not always be enough), and the random `DISPLAY_NUM` (`:90`-`:99`) colliding with a leftover Xvfb from a previous run of this same script in the same session.
- Severity: medium — makes the skill's own verification step an unreliable signal; following its documented advice ("a genuine FAIL always means the .scr's coordinates need to change") on a false FAIL would send someone chasing a nonexistent geometry bug.
- Status: open

- shparse2ast-absolute-path-function-crash: shparse2ast (/usr/local/bin/shparse2ast)
  given an absolute script path misbehaves on scripts that define a function:
  small ones die with a core dump (`timeout` reports "dumped core"), while
  bytes-0.4.12/ci/install.sh from the cargo registry makes it print JSON
  forever (filled the disk to 100% before it was killed). The same scripts
  parse fine when run from their own directory with a relative path, and
  scripts without a function definition are fine either way. Found while
  writing shell-commands.js, which now runs `cd dir && shparse2ast ./base`
  and caps the output size as a workaround; root cause in shparse2ast not
  investigated.

    printf 'f() { echo hi; }\nf\n' > /tmp/t.sh; shparse2ast -P /tmp/t.sh | head -c 100000 | wc -c

